<h2>Indian pulses for export</h2>
<p>India is both the world's largest producer and largest consumer of pulses, which shapes the export trade in a way buyers should understand: domestic demand comes first, and export availability for some pulses varies with the domestic supply position and the policy response to it.</p>

<p>What we export consistently is chickpeas (kabuli and desi), toor dal, moong, urad and masoor, machine-cleaned and sortex-sorted, whole or split.</p>

<h2>Specifying pulses</h2>
<p>The parameters that determine what lands in your warehouse:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type and form</strong> — whole or split (dal), polished or unpolished.</li>
  <li><strong>Count</strong> — for kabuli chickpeas, count per ounce (42-44, 58-60 and so on). Lower count means larger chickpeas and a price premium.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically ≤12%. Higher moisture is a storage-stability risk on a product that may sit in a warehouse for months.</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture and foreign matter</strong> — sortex-sorted grades hold much tighter tolerances.</li>
  <li><strong>Damaged, discoloured and split percentage</strong> — for whole pulses.</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — the percentage of the stated type in the lot.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Storage, fumigation and documentation</h2>
<p>Pulses are stored products, which means insect infestation is the main risk between packing and arrival. Fumigation is frequently required by destination markets and we arrange it with the certificate included in the documentation set. Beyond that, expect a phytosanitary certificate, certificate of origin and — on request — a certificate of analysis covering moisture, admixture and purity.</p>
